<div class="header"><h2>msg with other cations</h2></div>
<div class="body">

<p>The "M" in "MSG" is the <em>monosodium</em> &mdash; one sodium ion paired with the glutamate anion. The <strong>glutamate</strong> anion is what binds the umami receptors (T1R1/T1R3 and the metabotropic receptors mGluR1 and mGluR4), so the umami effect is not specific to sodium <sup><a href="#src-msg-alts-1">msg-alts-1</a>, <a href="#src-msg-alts-2">msg-alts-2</a></sup>. Swap the sodium for a different cation and you get a closely-related flavor enhancer that still tastes savory, but with a different secondary mineral note.</p>

<h3>known alternatives</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monopotassium glutamate (MPG, E622)</strong> &mdash; the most common non-sodium MSG alternative. Generally recognized as safe in both the EU and the USA, used as a flavor enhancer <sup><a href="#src-msg-alts-3">msg-alts-3</a></sup>. Tastes very similar to MSG; in psychophysical tests, potassium salts produce a perceptible umami taste just as sodium salts do <sup><a href="#src-msg-alts-1">msg-alts-1</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Calcium diglutamate (E623)</strong> &mdash; calcium salt of glutamic acid, approved as a flavor enhancer in the EU <sup><a href="#src-msg-alts-1">msg-alts-1</a></sup>. Milder onset than MSG.</li>
<li><strong>Monoammonium glutamate (E624)</strong> &mdash; ammonium salt, approved in the EU <sup><a href="#src-msg-alts-1">msg-alts-1</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Magnesium diglutamate (E625)</strong> &mdash; magnesium salt, approved in the EU <sup><a href="#src-msg-alts-1">msg-alts-1</a></sup>. Magnesium can taste bitter or mineral at higher concentrations, so it is less commonly used.</li>
</ul>

<p>All five glutamate salts (E620 through E625) are formally recognized as flavor enhancers in the EU and dissociate into the same free glutamate anion in aqueous solution, behaving identically to free glutamate in the body <sup><a href="#src-msg-alts-1">msg-alts-1</a>, <a href="#src-msg-alts-4">msg-alts-4</a></sup>. The sodium form (E621) is just the historically dominant commercial product.</p>

<h3>free glutamic acid itself</h3>
<p>Free L-glutamic acid (E620, no cation at all) also produces umami on its own. In taste tests it elicits a perceptible savory character, though it is often described as carrying more of a sour edge than the salt forms. The crystalline sodium salt became dominant in 1909 specifically because Ikeda found that replacing one hydrogen on the glutamate molecule with sodium "induce[d] pure umami without a sour taste" <sup><a href="#src-msg-alts-2">msg-alts-2</a></sup>.</p>

<h3>nucleotide enhancers have cation-swaps too</h3>
<p>Disodium 5'-inosinate (IMP, E631) and disodium 5'-guanylate (GMP, E627) are the two ribonucleotide co-enhancers of MSG; they are typically used together with MSG to produce a synergistic boost to umami (the E635 "disodium 5'-ribonucleotides" combination is the commercial shorthand). Both have dipotassium analogs (E632 dipotassium inosinate, E628 dipotassium guanylate) that are commercially available for low-sodium formulations <sup><a href="#src-dsg-history">dsg-history</a>, <a href="#src-dsg-history-3">dsg-history-3</a></sup>.</p>

<h3>why this matters for project achilles</h3>
<p>Project Achilles frames MSG around its sodium partner, and most of its criticisms target either the glutamate bolus or the sodium load &mdash; sometimes both. The chemistry above shows those are separable: a potassium-glutamate product would carry the umami with a much smaller sodium load (some of the sodium in MSG is replaced with potassium, which is the opposite of what most diets need), and a calcium- or ammonium-glutamate product would carry umami with essentially no sodium at all.</p>

<p><span class="highlight">The US FDA labeling rule is written specifically around "monosodium glutamate."</span> When MSG has been added to a food, the ingredient panel must list it as "monosodium glutamate" <sup><a href="#src-msg-alts-4">msg-alts-4</a></sup>. A potassium-glutamate or calcium-glutamate ingredient would fall outside that specific labeling requirement &mdash; the additive's name would appear, but it would not be labeled as "MSG." Whether the FDA treats these salts as substantively equivalent to MSG for labeling purposes is a separate regulatory question this section does not resolve; it is noted here only because the chemistry makes the loophole possible.</p>

<p>That loophole is interesting precisely because Project Achilles' biggest practical complaint is that <em>added</em> MSG never has to be labeled by the names most consumers would recognize as "the same thing" &mdash; yeast extract, hydrolyzed protein, natural flavor, and so on. The same labeling-by-specific-chemical-name logic that hides sodium glutamate behind ingredient-list technicalities also lets potassium glutamate walk past the "MSG" name entirely.</p>

